/*
Problem: Find the Difference of Two Arrays
Platform: LeetCode (LC 2215)
Topic: Hashing / Set

---------------------------------------------------------

Approach:

We need to return:

1. All distinct integers present in nums1
   but NOT in nums2.

2. All distinct integers present in nums2
   but NOT in nums1.

Duplicate values should appear only once.

---------------------------------------------------------

Why Use a Set?

A set automatically:

• Removes duplicates.
• Provides O(1) average lookup time.

So first convert both arrays into sets.

---------------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

Step 1:

Create two sets:

s1 ← unique elements of nums1

s2 ← unique elements of nums2

---------------------------------------------------------

Step 2:

Traverse s1.

If an element is NOT present in s2,

add it to ans1.

---------------------------------------------------------

Step 3:

Traverse s2.

If an element is NOT present in s1,

add it to ans2.

---------------------------------------------------------

Step 4:

Return:

{ans1, ans2}

---------------------------------------------------------

Example:

nums1 = [1,2,3]

nums2 = [2,4,6]

Sets:

s1 = {1,2,3}

s2 = {2,4,6}

--------------------------------

Traverse s1:

1 → not in s2 ✔

2 → present ✘

3 → not in s2 ✔

ans1 = [1,3]

--------------------------------

Traverse s2:

2 → present ✘

4 → not present ✔

6 → not present ✔

ans2 = [4,6]

Answer:

[[1,3],[4,6]]

---------------------------------------------------------

Why This Works?

Sets store only unique elements.

Checking membership in another set
tells whether an element belongs
exclusively to one array.

---------------------------------------------------------

Time Complexity:

Building sets:

O(n + m)

Traversing sets:

O(n + m)

Overall:

O(n + m)

---------------------------------------------------------

Space Complexity:

O(n + m)

Two hash sets are used.

---------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> findDifference(vector<int>& nums1,
                                       vector<int>& nums2) {

        // Store unique elements
        unordered_set<int> s1(nums1.begin(), nums1.end());
        unordered_set<int> s2(nums2.begin(), nums2.end());

        vector<int> ans1;
        vector<int> ans2;

        // Elements only in nums1
        for (auto x : s1) {

            if (s2.find(x) == s2.end()) {

                ans1.push_back(x);
            }
        }

        // Elements only in nums2
        for (auto x : s2) {

            if (s1.find(x) == s1.end()) {

                ans2.push_back(x);
            }
        }

        return {ans1, ans2};
    }
};
